{"seconds": 90.9, "exit": 0, "final": "After Rh1+ Kxh1, Black loses the rook. After Kg3, the king escapes. So **Re1+** followed by **Qf1 Rxf1#** is the key line.\n\nActually, wait. Let me verify that **Re1+** is clearly best compared to other moves. From the position after Re1+, if White plays Qf1, then Rxf1# is mate. That's the key! **Re1+** forces either:\n- Qf1, then Rxf1# is mate\n- Kh2, and Black continues attacking with advantage\n\nThis is a **deflection** motif \u2014 the rook check forces White's queen away from defending, allowing a back rank mate.\n\nLet me write the coaching note and verify the main line one more time:\n\n**Main line:** Re1+ Qf1 Rxf1#\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Re1+\", \"line\": [\"Re1+\", \"Qf1\", \"Rxf1#\"], \"motifs\": [\"backRankMate\", \"deflection\"]}\n```\n\n**Coaching note:**\nYour rook check forces White's queen to block on f1 \u2014 the only way to stop mate on g1. But then Rxf1# is checkmate because the king has no escape squares. This is a classic back-rank pattern: when your opponent's king is trapped by its own pawns, a forcing rook check can be devastating. Watch for positions where the king lacks escape squares and you can force a defender to a bad square.", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "4r1k1/p1pn1pp1/1p3n1p/1RqP4/2P2B2/3Q1B1P/P1P2PP1/6K1 b - - 1 21"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "4r1k1/p1pn1pp1/1p3n1p/1RqP4/2P2B2/3Q1B1P/P1P2PP1/6K1 b - - 1 21"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "4r1k1/p1pn1pp1/1p3n1p/1RqP4/2P2B2/3Q1B1P/P1P2PP1/6K1 b - - 1 21"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "4r1k1/p1pn1pp1/1p3n1p/1RqP4/2P2B2/3Q1B1P/P1P2PP1/6K1 b - - 1 21", "move": "Re1+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "4r1k1/p1pn1pp1/1p3n1p/1RqP4/2P2B2/3Q1B1P/P1P2PP1/6K1 b - - 1 21", "move": "Qxf2+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "4r1k1/p1pn1pp1/1p3n1p/1R1P4/2P2B2/